{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Surfshark VPN for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3060,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spacetime on its smallest scales is a \nseething ocean of black holes and wormholes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7560,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flickering into and out of existence—or \nso many physicists think has to be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12000,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case. But why should we take this spacetime foam \nseriously if we’ve never seen any evidence of it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24180,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A while ago we started talking about the \nfundamentalness of space—is it the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elementary stage that holds all of the complexity \nof physics? Or is it just the way our brains make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35880,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense of something that’s nothing like the space \nof our experience. Well, we have good reason to at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40980,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least doubt that the fundamental building block \nof space is just more space. And that’s because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46140,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our best theory of the nature of space—Einstein’s \ngeneral theory of relativity—is incomplete. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51900,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes into severe conflict with the other great \npillar of modern physics: quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56820,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve spoken in the past about the source of \nthis conflict, and about some of the speculative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62580,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solutions such as string theory and loop quantum \ngravity. In both of these theories, space gives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68100,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way to something rather different—something \nmore fundamental at the tiniest scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these theories have proved damnably difficult \nto test, and so we can’t say what the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79560,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elementary building block of space really is. But \nthat doesn’t mean we can’t say something about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what space looks like at close to the smallest \nscales. By using a little logical deduction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91680,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can combine general relativity and \nquantum mechanics just well enough to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96780,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trick nature into giving us an answer. And \nthat answer is that spacetime is … foamy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was John Archibald Wheeler who worked \nthis one out. Wheeler was a central figure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107640,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the development of both general relativity \nand quantum mechanics, and adviser to Richard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112380,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feynman and Hugh Everett and Kip Thorne and many \nmany others. Here’s the analogy that Wheeler came  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118080,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with to describe his conclusions about the \nminutest scales of the fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you're flying in a plane over the Atlantic \nocean. From a regular cruising altitude, looking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129600,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down, the surface of the ocean appears smooth \nand relatively featureless. But, if your plane  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135480,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lowered its altitude and got closer you may start \nto see the waves—from here just tiny disturbances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142260,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the surface of the water. But if you got \neven close, perhaps on a large ship sailing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the waters you'd see the foam and white \ncaps of waves forming and breaking, and you’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153840,
    "dDurationMs": 7860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feel their effect on your motion. And if you’re \nin a rowboat the wave would be everything—you’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161700,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be moving through a space that was far from flat, \nbut rather dominated by ever changing geometries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168120,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this analogy, the 2-D surface of \nthe ocean is 3-dimensional space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For large creatures like us humans, \nwe’re flying high above and see smooth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177960,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flat, and perfectly continuous spatial dimensions. \nBut “descending” closer to the Planck scale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184620,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we would see ubiquitous tiny fluctuations as \ndistances and geometries became warped. And at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Planck scale, spacetime would become \nso curved that black holes and wormholes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195240,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be popping into existence, only to vanish \nagain—as if our ocean were now violently boiling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201540,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is Wheeler’s “spacetime foam’’, and \nthere’s good reason to think that spacetime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206400,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really does look like this when you’re close to \nthe smallest scales—regardless of your preferred  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211020,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of quantum gravity. We need the latter \nto say what spacetime is made of—to understand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the analogous level of the water molecules in \nour ocean. However, the behavior of a quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223080,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime just above that level can perhaps be \nunderstood regardless of its underlying theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227580,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as we can describe the behavior \nof water without knowing its chemistry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231780,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to see where the \nidea of spacetime foam came from,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234780,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also how it might be \ntested. Now we'll do this by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239040,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combining two of the fundamental ideas in \neach theory of the fundamental theories the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244500,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty of quantum mechanics and \nthe geometry of general relativity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248940,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because spacetime foam is what you get \nwhen geometry itself becomes uncertain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255000,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the bit about uncertainty. \nThe Heisenberg uncertainty principle is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259620,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the foundational concepts in quantum \nmechanics. One version tells us that we can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneously know both the position and the \nmomentum of a particle to arbitrary precision.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270600,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The product of their uncertainties will always \nbe larger than a particular very small number."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276540,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a very deep relationship that we get \ninto in detail in this episode. But let me  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282840,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also remind you of a simpler way to think \nabout the Heisenberg uncertainty principle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287460,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we covered more recently—the Heisenberg \nmicroscope. Imagine you try to measure the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292680,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position of an object by bouncing a photon \noff it. You’ll get a more precise position  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297300,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement with a shorter wavelength photon. \nThat photon will transfer some of its momentum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the object, as will any measurement \nattempt. The shorter the wavelength,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306960,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more momentum the photon can transfer. \nSo the more precisely you measure position,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311640,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the less certain you become about \nthe final momentum of the object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315960,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a limit to the precision \nof our position measurement, even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319920,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re happy with absolute uncertainty in momentum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322740,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That limit is a consequence of us bringing general \nrelativity into the picture. GR tells us that mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328920,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and energy changes the geometry of space. This \nis described by the Einstein field equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334500,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the mass-energy content of space completely \ndetermining the geometry of that space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340740,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So as our measuring photon gains \nmomentum and therefore energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344520,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it introduces uncertainty into the geometry \nof space between you and the object,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349860,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which increases our uncertainty in the distance to \nthe object. So as the photon gets more energetic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357600,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Heisenberg uncertainty goes down but the \nuncertainty in the geometry goes up. They equal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364500,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other when the uncertainty is equal to the \nPlanck length, which is a tiny 1.6x10^-35 meters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372840,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the absolute limit of our ability to \npinpoint objects in space is the Planck length.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378900,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what does that mean for the \nstructure of space on that scale?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383880,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say we now want to measure the size of \na little block of space. The measuring photon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388620,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to have a wavelength at least \nsmaller than the width of that block.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That photon also warps our chunk of space. The \nEinstein field equations bv can tell us roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399240,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the introduced curvature is equal to the \nsize of the space we’re trying to measure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404460,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that if we try to measure the size \nof a 1-Planck-length block of space, the curvature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410700,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "introduced is equal to a Planck length. We \ncan express our curvature uncertainty with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417660,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new version of the uncertainty principle that \ncombines quantum mechanics and general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423420,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we have our first glimpse of the spacetime \nfoam. As we peer closer and closer at the fabric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime, we see that its curvature at \nany point becomes more and more variable;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435000,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its geometry more uncertain, \nuntil the curvature dominates.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440220,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does that mean? Well, geometries that have a \nradius of curvature similar to their size include  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446940,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spheres, cylinders, and black holes. So those are \nthe geometries that emerge at the Planck scale.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454620,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space at that scale curves completely back \non itself, infinitesimal black holes appear,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460620,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and wormholes connect nearby regions. But such \nPlanck scale black holes would likely rapidly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467820,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evaporate due to Hawking radiation and the \nwormholes would be unstable and collapse in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472380,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a very short amount of time. The quantum foam \nis as transitory as any quantum fluctuation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To better understand the dynamics of \nthe spacetime foam, let’s think about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482760,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum fluctuations. For that it’s \nhelpful to turn to the other popular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487680,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "version of the Heisenberg uncertainty \nprinciple, which tells us that we can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491340,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneously know both the energy \nand the time or duration of an event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497940,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you try to measure the energy \nof a system at a very precise time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503040,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or over a very short duration, then your \nenergy measurement becomes highly uncertain.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509880,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if energy can be fundamentally \nuncertain, that means there can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a fundamental uncertainty in the physical \ncontents of the universe. To understand that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let me give you a quick refresher on our best \ntheory of stuff in the universe. Quantum field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524400,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory and in particular the standard model of \nparticle physics nicely describes all matter and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529140,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 of the 4 forces as excitations in different \nquantum fields that permeate all of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535740,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because of the time-energy uncertainty \nprinciple, we can never know the exact energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541140,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "present in a particular patch of space. We can’t \neven know that the energy is precisely zero even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547920,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the complete absence of real particles. What \nshould be a complete vacuum might be observed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554760,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have non-zero energy, and that becomes more likely \nthe shorter the timescale of our observation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560340,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This manifests as an underlying buzz of rapid \nenergy fluctuations in the quantum vacuum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is sometimes described as an ocean of \nvirtual particles constantly appearing and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vanishing, although it’s way more complicated than \nthat. Now, this isn’t quite our spacetime foam,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577980,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it says nothing about the shape \nof the fabric of spacetime on its own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581880,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s bring general relativity back \ninto it. This fluctuating energy of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586500,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum can be thought of as an uncertainty \nin whatever’s sitting on the right side  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591540,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Einstein equations. The mass-energy \ncontent of a tiny chunk of space is uncertain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596820,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the corresponding geometry should be uncertain \nalso. If we think about this in terms of virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles, then every time one appears, we should \nsee an accompanying tiny gravitational field that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609960,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lasts just for an instant. But because these \nquantum fluctuations are extremely complex and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615120,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly changing, the effect on the geometry \nof space is also complex and ever-changing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620400,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Virtual particles aren’t even restricted to the \nnormal rules that real particles have—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625740,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative masses —which means exotic \nobjects like wormholes can appear and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631140,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vanish amid the general roiling mess \nof spacetime at the Planck scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635700,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because these quantum fluctuations are \nactually in a superposition of many different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states, we need to think of the geometry \nof spacetime as also being in a quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645840,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition at that scale. Many, and perhaps \nevery possible geometry exists simultaneously.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we see on the macroscopic \nscale is the washed out sum of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657720,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all Planck-scale configurations—and \nit ends up conveniently nice and flat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662940,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of the quantum foam as \nbeing due to an intrinsic uncertainty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667200,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in either the stuff space contains or \nin the geometry of spacetime itself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672720,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are two ways to get at the same conclusion: \nas long as the uncertainty principle holds in some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678180,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reasonable way for gravity, then on the Planck \nscale the fabric of spacetime should be foamy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684240,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now we have a picture of what space might look \nlike on scales well below our experience, or even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689220,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below the experience of atoms or nuclei. How do we \ntest this? If the standard picture is right, then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695820,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum fluctuations of spacetime only \nbecome significant near the Planck scale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700440,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is far, far smaller than any of our \ndirect probes of spacetime structure—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705540,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’ll probably be the case until we’re \na super-advanced galactic civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710880,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But indirect tests are possible now. Remember \nWheeler’s analogy of spacetime foam as a choppy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718020,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean surface. A gigantic ship doesn’t \nnotice the waves, but a rowboat does. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723900,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vessel in between may have a relatively smooth \nride, but might be deflected or impeded over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730260,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time on a wavy ocean versus a still one. If \nspacetime foam is real, then we might expect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736260,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particle traveling very long distances \nto experience a slight shift in its course."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742440,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we can test this with a surprisingly \nsimple experiment. A distant point of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747660,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light can produce diffraction patterns, for \nexample these diffraction spikes and airy rings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753480,
    "dDurationMs": 8220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we observe through a telescope aperture or \ndiffraction fringes through a pair of slits. Those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761700,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns are due to individual photons interfering \nwith themselves after interacting with the gap.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768600,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Photons that reach us traveling in the same \ndirection will experience the same interference.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775740,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By catching many such photons, we slowly \nbuild up a single interference pattern.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781260,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But two photons coming at even slightly \ndifferent angles will land on our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detector according to different interference \npatterns. If we look at many such photons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those different patterns will be overlaid \non each other and result in just a blur."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796080,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why we see diffraction spikes \naround stars in Hubble or even JWST images,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803760,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we only see a blurry blob from even our best \nground-based telescopes. In the latter case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809820,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light from the star is bounced around by our \natmosphere so that it arrives traveling in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815940,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly different directions. Well, if spacetime \nis foamy then we might expect a similar effect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821400,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for light arriving from very great distances. \nStars in our own galaxy are not far enough away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828540,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but some objects like quasars and gamma ray \nbursts can be billions of light years distant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of studies have tried to detect the \neffect of spacetime foam on the diffraction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839640,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns of distant objects in Hubble Space \nTelescope data. Hubble is particularly good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845100,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it’s very sensitive to ultraviolet \nlight—a short wavelength light that should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849960,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be more influenced by a turbulent spacetime \nfabric compared to, say, the long-wavelength  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855780,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared light that JWST is sensitive to. So \nfar the best these studies have been able to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do is to rule out certain models which produce a \nparticularly strong spacetime foam. But some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865800,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the measurements are tantalizingly close to \nthe needed sensitivity, so perhaps a bigger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871500,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultra-violet-sensitive space telescope will be \nable to detect this effect. More work is needed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877500,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only then could we really be \nsure that a tempestuous ocean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881220,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of uncertain geometries underlies \nour deceptively placid spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886320,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Surshark VPN for supporting PBS. \nSurfshark VPN is a virtual private network that  "
    } ]
  }, {
